/-!
# The braiding functors preserve perfect complexes

B. Webster, *Knot invariants and higher representation theory*, arXiv:1309.3796v3, Lemma 6.13,
in the ungraded reading of Definition 6.6: the derived braiding functor
`𝔅((1, …, ℓ), w) = - ⊗ᴸ B((1, …, ℓ), w)` sends compact objects of the derived category of the
corner at the red order `(1, …, ℓ)` to compact objects of that of the corner at `w(1, …, ℓ)`
(`isCompact_braidingFunctor_obj`). In the derived category of a ring the compact objects are the
perfect complexes (`DG.DegreeZero.isCompact_iff_isPerfect`).

The argument is Webster's. The functor sends the regular module to `B((1, …, ℓ), w)` in degree
`0`, which has a filtration by loading standards (Lemma 6.11). Each loading standard is a cyclic
module over a Noetherian ring (the corner is finite-dimensional) with finite projective dimension
(Corollary 5.22), so it is perfect.

Webster works with graded modules and the bounded above derived category; here the functor is
the ungraded one of `braidingFunctor`, on the unbounded derived category.

Route hypothesis: that of Corollary 6.10 (`RowFigureFiveHypotheses`), through Lemma 6.11.
-/

noncomputable section

